From 30e1dd3597068bbd20d30b6bba680d7e047ad662 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 01:55:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: nf_tables: say when a commit is still being
 applied

A commit bumps net->nft.base_seq and flips the generation cursor, then
goes on applying the rest of the transaction: base chain policies,
element timeouts, the set backends' commit updates. Something outside
nf_tables that caches a verdict for a generation cannot tell when that
is over. The commit mutex and the gc_seq busy mark are private to the
pernet state, whose id is not exported, and nfnetlink releases its
subsystem lock before the batch runs.

Add net->nft.commit_applying, set just before base_seq is published and
cleared with a release store once the set updates are in, and
nft_commit_in_progress() to read it with an acquire. A reader that has
seen a commit's base_seq with smp_load_acquire() and then finds it
clear sees that commit fully applied. The field sits in the padding
after gencursor, so struct net does not grow, and the reader is an
inline load with no dependency on the nf_tables module.

An offload that keys streams on their addresses alone -- routed
multicast, carried under source and group -- also needs to know whether
the ruleset could tell two such streams apart by their ports: once one
is seen forwarded, the others ride the same entry. Add
nft_port_dependent(), which judges that from the ruleset without a
packet, bracketed by base_seq and the applying mark. It walks netdev
ingress up the input device's stack, prerouting (NAT chains included),
forward, postrouting and netdev egress down each output's stack, in hook
order, reading each chain's rule blob for the current generation as
nft_do_chain() does, with everything about the stream known but its
ports. An expression that reads the ports, a limit or quota, a lookup in
a set that changes without a commit, all split the walk. The answer is 0
only when every path accepts at every hook, for every output, with the
same effects, a conntrack helper among them, and no translation -- an
address-keyed offload applies none, and one of the ports alone leaves
the addresses as they were; a path that drops, anything the walk does
not model, a bridge family chain on a bridged path, and a device in
either stack that is not Ethernet, or is but has no parent or lower
device -- whose underlay the walk cannot follow -- make it 1. The
stream's first packet is followed, then later ones without the NAT
chains and with the conntrack mark the earlier one left. It lives in
nf_tables because the per-net table list, where NAT chains are found, is
private to it.

For it to read them, the private data of cmp, range, bitwise, byteorder,
lookup and objref maps moves to nf_tables_core.h, that of nat, masq and
redir to a new nft_nat.h, and the ct expression records whether it
stores into the conntrack, which its ops do not tell apart. A set a
dynset binds to is marked as updated from the packet path, which its
flags need not say.

x_tables can tell such streams apart too, and its hooks being there
says nothing: a router's own iptables-legacy FORWARD and NAT rules would
refuse every stream. Add nf_xt_port_dependent(), which walks the
ip_tables or ip6_tables tables a forwarded copy crosses at prerouting,
forward and postrouting as ipt_do_table() would, for all the stream's
packets at once, inside an xt_recseq section: a rule whose header
matches applies, one with a match may or may not, and a match that does
more than decide, or any verdict or target but ACCEPT, RETURN to an
accepting policy, LOG, NFLOG and TRACE on a path, makes the answer 1.
ip_tables and ip6_tables publish their walkers through RCU pointers in
the netfilter core, so the caller depends on neither. Their hooks are
typed NF_HOOK_OP_XTABLES, mangle running its table through a wrapper of
its own, and the NAT core's NF_HOOK_OP_NAT: the iptables-legacy NAT
tables are registered with it and found only through its private data,
which moves to nf_nat.h. The NAT core now frees its hook ops after a
grace period, as it already freed that data, and the registration error
paths that unregister hooks again -- ip_tables', ip6_tables', the NAT
core's and the NAT tables' own -- wait one before freeing what they
named: a reader of the hook list under RCU, the probe as much as the
hook dump, may hold a pointer into them. The pure-match and
passing-target lists live in x_tables.c, for both families.

An x_tables table has no generation a reader could compare. Add
net->nf.xt_seq, moved with a release by every table registration, by
every replacement once xt_replace_table() has waited out the packets
reading the table it replaced, and by every removal, so that a caller
that cached the answer asks again when it moves.

Signed-off-by: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
